=== Layer 5-7 - The Application Layer

==== Why the upper three layers collapse

The OSI model formally separates Session (5), Presentation (6), and Application (7) into three distinct layers. In real protocols, this separation does not hold. There is no PDU transformation between them, no analogue to Ethernet → IP or IP → TCP where the data unit changes name and a new header is built. An application takes a TCP segment or a UDP datagram, places its data inside, and that is the entire dependency relationship for most protocols on the wire today. The application is not built on top of a session protocol that is built on top of a presentation protocol. It is built directly on top of a transport segment.

The reason is that each application makes its own decisions about session management, encoding, compression, and encryption. BGP runs over TCP and treats the TCP connection itself as the session. SMTP defines its own session state inside the protocol, `HELO`, `MAIL FROM`, `RCPT TO`, `QUIT`. HTTP/1.1 has no session at all; every request is independent and the server-side state is reconstructed from cookies or tokens carried in headers. HTTP/2 reinvents a session as multiplexed streams within a single TCP connection. There is no common session-layer protocol that every application uses. The same is true of presentation, some applications define their own wire format, others use whatever serialisation library they happen to depend on, others inherit a format from their substrate. There is no presentation-layer protocol common to every application.

The one near-universal cross-cutting standard is *TLS*. When an application uses TLS, the connection can meaningfully be decomposed into the sublayers the model describes, the TLS handshake provides session establishment (Layer 5), the Record Protocol provides encoding and confidentiality (Layer 6), and the application protocol sits above (Layer 7). The protocol classification places TLS as a Composite Inter-Layer protocol exactly for this reason: it bundles handshake, record encryption, and alert sub-protocols into a single named thing that spans the upper three layers and depends on a transport layer below. When an application does not use TLS, no such decomposition exists. The application talks directly to TCP, and the layers collapse into a single application layer that does whatever it chooses to do internally.

BGP is the working example. The protocol as standardised runs over plain TCP and is, in the classification scheme, a Single Layer (Layer 7) protocol. There is no session sublayer because the TCP connection is the session. There is no presentation sublayer because BGP defines its own message format directly. There is no encryption. The increasingly-discussed proposals for BGP over TLS would change this: BGP-over-TLS would become a Composite Inter-Layer protocol, and the upper-three-layer decomposition would become meaningful. Today, for the vast majority of BGP deployments, it is not. The same protocol, with and without TLS, sits in two different categories. This is the pattern across the application layer: the layer structure is not a property of the model; it is a property of the choices each application makes.

==== What is actually cross-cutting

TLS is uniquely dominant, but it is not the only cross-cutting standard worth knowing. A handful of others appear repeatedly across application protocols and deserve naming explicitly, because architectural decisions about authentication, encoding, and substrate often turn on which of these an application has chosen to depend on.

*SASL, the Simple Authentication and Security Layer* (RFC 4422) is a framework for plugging authentication mechanisms into application protocols without each protocol having to invent its own. SMTP uses it for `AUTH`. IMAP and POP3 use it. LDAP uses it. XMPP uses it. Kafka uses it. The mechanisms underneath SASL, `PLAIN`, `LOGIN`, `SCRAM-SHA-256`, `EXTERNAL`, `GSSAPI`, are themselves pluggable, which means an application that supports SASL can in principle support any authentication mechanism that has been packaged for it. The architectural significance is that SASL turns authentication into a swappable component rather than a hard-coded protocol feature, which is the right shape for it.

*GSSAPI, the Generic Security Services API* (RFC 2743) plays a similar role for the Kerberos ecosystem. SSH, NFS, SMB, and LDAP all support GSSAPI authentication, which in practice means Kerberos authentication. Where an organisation has a Kerberos infrastructure already, GSSAPI is the mechanism through which application protocols hook into it.

*ASN.1 with DER encoding* is an older presentation-layer standard that remains load-bearing in some critical places. X.509 certificates are ASN.1. LDAP is ASN.1. SNMP is ASN.1. Kerberos messages are ASN.1. The encoding is verbose, the parsers are historically a source of bugs, and modern applications generally prefer JSON or protocol buffers, but anything that touches certificates or directory services touches ASN.1 whether the developer knows it or not.

*MIME, Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions* (RFC 2045 and surrounding) began as a way to encode non-ASCII content in email and grew into the general content-typing standard for HTTP and any protocol that needed to label the type of data it was carrying. The `Content-Type` header is MIME. Multipart message structures are MIME. S/MIME for encrypted email is MIME. Wherever an application has to declare "what kind of data is this," MIME types are usually the vocabulary.

*HTTP itself* has become cross-cutting in a different sense. It is no longer just one application protocol among many, it has become the substrate for many other application protocols. REST APIs are HTTP. gRPC runs over HTTP/2. GraphQL and JSON-RPC ride HTTP. WebSocket is established by an HTTP upgrade. OAuth 2.0 flows are HTTP redirects. CalDAV and CardDAV are HTTP. The architectural consequence is that "speaks HTTP" has become a near-universal capability and that organisations now run dozens of distinct application protocols all of which look, on the wire, like HTTP traffic to TCP port 443. This collapses the network-level visibility the older "one port per service" model offered, and shifts the meaningful policy enforcement points up into the application layer.

*OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect* are an authorisation and identity layer specifically for HTTP-based services. They are not universally cross-application, they belong to the HTTP family, but within that family they are the dominant standard, and an organisation with many web applications is almost certainly going to authenticate them through some OAuth/OIDC provider rather than have each application maintain its own user database. The Identity chapter takes these up properly; the architectural point at this layer is that they exist and that they are how application authentication is actually done in practice.

That is the genuinely cross-cutting list. Beyond it, the application layer is a long catalogue of protocols each of which makes its own choices. TLS is the only standard that crosses essentially every application family.

==== TLS, the load-bearing protocol

TLS deserves its own treatment because it is, in practice, what makes the application layer possible to operate over a network the architecture cannot trust. The introduction's commitment to encrypted-by-default internal traffic is carried in the lower layers by an encrypted mesh VPN; on the wider internet and at the application boundary, that work is done by TLS. The two layers of encryption are not redundant, they protect different things, terminate at different points, and are operated by different parties, but they are the same kind of answer to the same kind of problem: the medium between the endpoints cannot be assumed to be private, so the data on the medium must be encrypted with keys held by the endpoints themselves.

What TLS provides, when used correctly, is three properties. *Confidentiality*: an observer of the encrypted connection cannot read the application data. *Integrity*: an observer who modifies bytes in transit will cause the receiver to detect the modification and abort the connection. *Authentication*: at minimum, the client verifies the server's identity through a certificate signed by a trusted authority; with mutual TLS (mTLS), the server also verifies the client's identity the same way.

The third property is the one organisations underuse. Conventional TLS deployment authenticates the server to the client and treats the client as anonymous, with application-layer authentication (a username and password, an API key, an OAuth token) layered on top to identify the client. mTLS replaces the client-side authentication step with a certificate, which means the client's identity is established cryptographically before any application-layer exchange happens. The architectural significance is large. An mTLS-protected service refuses connections from clients without a valid certificate at the TLS handshake, before the application even sees the request. Anonymous reconnaissance of the service becomes impossible. Credential theft of an application-layer secret no longer grants access on its own; the attacker needs the certificate and the corresponding private key, both of which can live in hardware that does not expose the key to software. mTLS is the closest TLS gets to fail-safe defaults at the application boundary.

TLS has a long history of failures, and the failures are instructive because they reflect the complexity of the protocol. Heartbleed was an implementation flaw in OpenSSL's handling of the TLS Heartbeat extension, a memory-disclosure bug that exposed private keys, session data, and any other contents of process memory to anyone who could send the malformed extension. BEAST exploited a weakness in the way TLS 1.0 chained CBC-mode block cipher encryption between records. CRIME and BREACH exploited compression performed inside the TLS layer, allowing attackers to recover secrets through a side channel. FREAK and Logjam exploited downgrade attacks against weak export-grade cipher suites that should have been removed from the protocol decades earlier. Every one of these attacks targeted a specific sub-protocol or a specific implementation choice within the composite that TLS is. None of them broke the underlying cryptography. They broke the bundling.

The version that should be deployed today is *TLS 1.3*. It removes the older cipher suites that gave rise to most of the historical attacks, eliminates the renegotiation mechanism that was the source of several others, removes compression entirely, and simplifies the handshake from two round trips to one. It is, in protocol-design terms, a substantial reduction in attack surface. Every earlier version, TLS 1.2, 1.1, 1.0, and the SSL versions before them, should be disabled at every endpoint the organisation controls. Not because they are catastrophically broken in every deployment, but because their continued support requires the implementation to maintain code paths that have produced attacks before and could produce them again. Economy of mechanism applies: the simpler the configuration, the smaller the attack surface, and the fewer places a misconfiguration can hide.

The hard part of operating TLS is not the cryptography. It is *certificate management*. Every endpoint that terminates TLS needs a certificate. The certificate has to be issued by a CA the other side trusts. It has to be renewed before it expires. The private key has to be protected. When a key is compromised, the certificate has to be revoked, and the revocation has to be honoured by clients, which historically has been the weakest link in the chain. The Identity chapter takes this up in proper depth, including the open-source PKI primitives, `step-ca` for an internal CA, ACME for automated issuance, `cert-manager` for in-cluster automation, that allow certificate management to be expressed as infrastructure-as-code rather than as a manual operation. The architectural commitment at this layer is that certificate management *is* infrastructure, and an architecture in which certificates are renewed by hand on production servers under time pressure is an architecture that will eventually have an outage caused by an expired certificate.

==== The course's position on break-and-inspect, applied here

The introduction rejected the practice of TLS-terminating proxies and break-and-inspect as a category. The application layer is where this rejection has to be made operational, because the application layer is where the temptation is greatest. WAFs want to inspect the plaintext of HTTP requests. DLP appliances want to scan outgoing data for sensitive content. Anti-malware platforms want to scan downloaded files. Each of these tools operates by terminating TLS at the network edge, examining the plaintext, and re-encrypting onward to the real destination. Each of them therefore concentrates plaintext, private keys, and onward credentials in a single appliance whose compromise yields access to everything.

The position taken by this course is the same here as in the introduction: do not terminate TLS in the middle of the path. TLS is end-to-end or it is not meaningful. The right answer is to deploy the controls that need plaintext access *at the endpoints*, on the host that produces or consumes the data, rather than at the network. A host-based WAF integrated with the application server inspects the same plaintext the application sees, with no separate decryption step, no separate concentration of cryptographic material, and no architectural component sitting in the trust path that the architect would otherwise prefer not to trust. A DLP control on the endpoint is in the same position. Egress filtering at the network can still be useful, restricting *which* destinations a host can reach is a Layer 3 / Layer 4 decision and does not require decryption, but inspecting *what* a host is sending requires being on the host.

This argument extends to the family of products marketed as "TLS visibility solutions" or "encrypted traffic inspection." The architecture-first response is that the visibility being sought is available without decryption: the metadata of TLS connections, which certificates are presented, which SNI values are seen, which destinations are reached, with what timing and what volume, is observable at the network layer and is rich enough to support meaningful detection. The Observability chapter develops this. Decrypting traffic at a midpoint to recover data that the network was deliberately designed not to expose is, as the introduction said, a cure worse than the disease.

==== DNS, the application protocol every other depends on

DNS is an application-layer protocol, but it occupies a special position because almost every other application connection begins with a DNS lookup. An application asks "what IP address corresponds to this hostname?", DNS answers, and the application opens a connection to the returned address. DNS is a Single Layer (Layer 7) protocol that sits underneath every other Layer 7 protocol's connection setup, which makes its trustworthiness a prerequisite for the trustworthiness of every other application connection.

Classical DNS has none of the security properties an architect would want. Queries and responses are sent in cleartext over UDP, with no authentication of the response. Anyone on the network path can read the query (revealing what the host is trying to reach) and forge a response (redirecting the host to an attacker-chosen address). The application receives an IP address, opens a TCP connection to it, and presents whatever credentials or requests the application protocol calls for, to the wrong server. This is the attack that cache-poisoning and DNS-hijacking variants have exploited for decades.

Two families of mechanisms address the two distinct problems. *DNSSEC* addresses authentication: every record in a DNSSEC-signed zone is accompanied by a cryptographic signature, traceable through a chain of trust to the DNS root. A resolver that validates DNSSEC can detect a forged response. The mechanism has been deployable for years and is widely supported in resolvers; the slow point is publication, the proportion of zones that are actually signed is still well below where it should be. Where the organisation controls a zone, signing it is the right default. Where the organisation is consuming external zones, configuring the resolver to validate is the right default.

*DoH (DNS over HTTPS)* and *DoT (DNS over TLS)* address confidentiality and integrity in transit. They wrap DNS queries inside TLS, which prevents observers on the network from reading the queries and prevents tampering with the responses. They do not by themselves provide the response-authentication property that DNSSEC does, a malicious resolver can still lie even over an encrypted channel, but they protect the path between the host and the resolver, which is the segment most exposed to opportunistic interception. The architectural choice is to run an internal validating resolver, *Unbound* is the open-source default, that validates DNSSEC on the way to authoritative servers, and to have all hosts use that resolver over an encrypted protocol. Unbound supports DoT and DoH directly. Hosts can be configured to use it through systemd-resolved or `dnscrypt-proxy` or any of several other open-source clients.

The combined model is straightforward: hosts query an internal resolver over an encrypted channel; the internal resolver validates DNSSEC on every response; the resolver's outbound queries to authoritative servers go over the public internet with whatever protections the authoritative servers support. The trust the host places in DNS is now reduced to the trust it places in the internal resolver, which the organisation operates. This is the same pattern the rest of the architecture follows, collapse external trust dependencies into a small number of internal components the organisation controls and can verify.

==== HTTP as a substrate

Many of the application protocols an organisation runs today are not really separate protocols on the wire. They are HTTP. A REST API is HTTP. A gRPC service is HTTP/2 with a binary message format. A GraphQL endpoint is HTTP. A WebSocket connection is established with an HTTP upgrade and then carries arbitrary message framing. SAML and OAuth flows are HTTP redirects. CalDAV and CardDAV are HTTP. WebDAV is HTTP. JMAP, the modern email-access protocol, is HTTP.

The architectural consequence is that the network-layer visibility the older model offered, different ports for different services, with port-based filtering as a meaningful policy mechanism, has largely collapsed at the upper layer. Almost everything the organisation runs is HTTPS to TCP port 443. The network sees a stream of TLS connections to a domain name, and the question of whether a particular connection is a legitimate application call, an exfiltration attempt, an OAuth callback, or a malware C2 channel is not answerable at the network layer alone.

This is one of the reasons the trust model in this course is pushed up the stack rather than left at the network. The network can no longer carry the trust decisions. The decisions live in the application, which clients are allowed to call which endpoints, with what credentials, performing what operations on what data. The Identity chapter develops the credential side; the Application Security chapter develops the input-validation and authorisation side. At this layer the architectural fact to absorb is that "is this allowed?" is no longer a question the firewall can answer for the upper layers. The firewall can answer "is this destination reachable?" The application has to answer "is this caller authorised to do this thing?" And the application has to answer it correctly, every time, because there is no longer a meaningful network control that catches the cases it gets wrong.

The corollary is that the application layer's security depends on the discipline with which authentication and authorisation are implemented. An application that accepts an OAuth token but does not verify its scope, or a gRPC service that authenticates a peer but does not check what operation the peer is allowed to invoke, is a service whose protections do not match the architectural commitment the rest of the stack is making. mTLS at the transport layer establishes that the caller is who they claim to be. The application layer has to enforce what the caller is allowed to do once they are confirmed. Both are required; neither substitutes for the other.

==== The application is the endpoint

The cryptography chapter made the point that cryptography protects data between endpoints, and that the endpoints themselves, the systems that legitimately hold the keys, are outside the protection. At the application layer, this point becomes operational. The application is the endpoint. The application sees plaintext. The application is the place where the cryptographic guarantees of every layer below terminate, and where the actual work the network exists to support is performed.

This is what makes application compromise more consequential than network-layer compromise. An attacker who compromises the network sees ciphertext between encrypted endpoints. An attacker who compromises the application sees the plaintext the application is processing, the credentials the application is using to authenticate to other services, and the data the application is writing to its database. The architecture cannot encrypt around an attacker who has gained execution within the application itself. The defences against application compromise are the host-level controls of the Endpoint chapter, least functionality, declarative configuration, mandatory access control, attestation, software supply chain discipline, and the application-level controls of the Application Security chapter, input validation, parameterised queries, output encoding, safe deserialisation, dependency hygiene.

The application layer is also where most of the organisation's code lives. The kernel might be ten million lines; the application stack is usually ten times that, counting libraries and dependencies. Every line is a potential vulnerability. Every dependency is a trust decision. Every update is a window in which something that was not previously in the trusted computing base becomes part of it. The xz/liblzma compromise of 2024, a maintainer takeover that introduced a backdoor into a library used by SSH on most Linux distributions, was an application-layer supply chain attack of the kind the SBOM-and-VEX pipeline described in the introduction is designed to detect, and it serves as a continuing reminder that the application layer's trust surface is mostly inherited rather than written.

==== The proliferation problem

Layers 1 through 4 use a small, well-known set of protocols. Ethernet, IP, TCP, UDP, ICMP, ARP, OSPF or BGP, TLS, and a handful of others account for nearly all the network traffic in any organisation. The set of *applications* an organisation runs is much larger. An ordinary small-to-medium business might run, on a typical day, mail servers, calendar servers, file servers, a VCS, an issue tracker, a wiki, a chat platform, an identity provider, a directory, a database or several, a monitoring system, a logging pipeline, several internal web applications, a customer-facing web application, a build system, a deployment system, and dozens of SaaS subscriptions on top. Each is an application. Each has its own configuration, its own authentication, its own update cycle, its own attack surface, its own failure modes, and its own log format.

This is where the introduction's warning about complexity hits hardest. Twenty-seven security products do not make a network more secure than ten products applied correctly. The same logic applies to applications. An organisation that runs forty-seven internal web applications, half of them deployed years ago by people who no longer work there, with overlapping function and inconsistent authentication, has not built a productive environment. It has built an attack surface that no one fully understands. The architectural answer is the same as elsewhere in the course: choose few applications, choose them carefully, justify each one's presence, prefer applications that share a common authentication and identity layer, and treat the deployment of a new application as a security-relevant decision rather than a routine one.

The applications that survive this filter should themselves be deployable from declarative configuration. The host that runs them is described in NixOS or Ansible. The application's own configuration files are version-controlled. The certificates it uses are issued automatically and rotated. The secrets it consumes come from a secrets store rather than a static file. The metrics it emits, the logs it writes, the traces it produces all flow into the shared observability pipeline. An application that meets these criteria is one the architecture can reason about. An application that does not is an artisanal hand-tuned exception, and every artisanal hand-tuned exception is a place where the next outage or compromise will originate.

==== Vendor applications and the control property

The Parkerian control property, the capacity to determine what happens to a system or to data, is tested most acutely at the application layer. This is where SaaS lives. This is where the closed-source enterprise applications live. This is where the organisation's own data is most likely to be sitting on infrastructure operated by someone else, processed by software the organisation cannot inspect, and protected by controls the organisation has to take on faith.

The position the course takes is the same as in the introduction: vendor dependence is a loss of control that has to be justified, not assumed. At the application layer the question is sharper because the vendors are more numerous and the data is more sensitive. Every SaaS subscription is a decision to put some category of data outside the organisation's direct control. Sometimes the trade is correct, the organisation is not in the business of building its own document collaboration platform, and the operational cost of self-hosting one would exceed the value of the additional control. Sometimes the trade is wrong, the organisation is using a third-party password manager, secrets store, or identity provider for credentials that are themselves the basis for accessing everything else, and the consequence of compromise at the vendor would be uncontainable. The architect's job is to make the trade explicitly, on a per-application basis, with the consequences of vendor compromise considered rather than assumed away.

For applications where in-house operation is feasible, the open-source landscape is now mature enough that most categories have a credible self-hosted option. Mail can be *Postfix* with *Dovecot*. Calendar and contacts can be *Radicale* or *Baikal*. File sharing can be *Nextcloud* or *Seafile*. Chat can be *Matrix* with *Synapse* or *Dendrite*. Code hosting can be *Gitea* or *Forgejo*. Issue tracking can be *Plane* or one of the alternatives. Wikis can be *BookStack* or *Outline* or *MediaWiki*. Identity can be *Keycloak* or *Authentik* or *Zitadel*. Each of these is deployable from declarative configuration, can be operated by a small team, and keeps the organisation in possession of its own data. None of them is free in the sense of zero operational cost, every self-hosted application has to be run, updated, and monitored, but each restores the control property the equivalent SaaS would have surrendered.

Where vendor applications must be used, because the organisation lacks the operational capacity to self-host, because no comparable open-source option exists, or because the vendor relationship offers something the organisation cannot reproduce, the introduction's standards apply: auditable behaviour, meaningful transparency including SBOMs, reproducible builds where possible, clear contractual and architectural limits on what the vendor's software can do on the organisation's systems. A vendor that cannot meet those standards is one whose presence in the trust path has to be questioned regardless of market position.

==== The architecture-first answer at Layer 7

The pattern from earlier chapters carries through. The architecture-first answer at the application layer is not a defensive technology bolted on top of insecure applications; it is a set of choices about which applications to run, how to authenticate to them, how to authorise actions within them, how to encrypt traffic to them, and how to operate them as code rather than as artefacts.

*Few applications, chosen carefully.* Each application is a trust decision. The default for a new application is "no" until the function it serves has been justified, the alternatives have been considered, and the operational cost has been accounted for. The architecture should be able to enumerate every application it runs and explain why each one is there.

*Open source where possible, vendor where necessary, with the trade made explicitly.* Self-hosted open-source applications restore the control property and place the organisation's data on infrastructure the organisation understands. Vendor applications are accepted when the trade is genuinely worth it, and rejected when it is not.

*Authentication centralised, not distributed.* The Identity chapter develops the substrate; at this layer the architectural commitment is that applications federate to a single identity provider rather than each maintaining its own user database. Federation through OIDC is the default for HTTP-based applications. SAML where federation requires it. LDAP via SASL where applications require it. Local accounts on each application are the exception, used only where federation is structurally impossible.

*Authorisation enforced in the application, evaluated correctly.* Authentication establishes who the caller is; authorisation determines what they are allowed to do. The application is the only place this can be enforced. Patterns that delegate authorisation to a network appliance are not enforcement; they are advisory hints that an attacker who reaches the application directly will bypass.

*TLS everywhere, terminating at the endpoint that needs the data.* Inside the organisation, the encrypted mesh VPN carries one layer of encryption between hosts; TLS to the application carries another layer between the client and the service. Both terminate at endpoints the organisation controls. Neither terminates at a midpoint appliance. mTLS is preferred where both sides of the connection are systems the organisation operates, because it makes the connection's authentication a property of cryptography rather than of credentials in a header.

*No break-and-inspect, anywhere.* The controls that need plaintext access run on the endpoints, not in the network. WAFs and DLP run on the application server. Egress filtering at the network restricts destinations rather than inspecting contents. Detection on encrypted traffic uses the available metadata, which is rich enough.

*DNS validated and encrypted.* An internal validating resolver, queried by all hosts over an encrypted channel, is the default. Where the organisation publishes zones, those zones are signed.

*Applications described as code.* Configuration in version control. Certificates issued automatically. Secrets sourced from a secrets store. Logs and metrics flowing to the observability pipeline. Updates applied through the same mechanism that deployed the application initially. An application that requires hand-editing a configuration file on a production host has not yet been brought under infrastructure as code.

*The supply chain treated as part of the application.* SBOMs generated for each application, fed into the same vulnerability-correlation pipeline that covers the operating system. Updates evaluated in light of provenance, who built this, from what source, with what signatures. Pinned dependencies, reproducible builds where the upstream supports them, awareness of the trust each new dependency adds.

These commitments produce an application layer that is small, comprehensible, and reason-able-about. They do not eliminate every application-layer risk, the application code itself can still have vulnerabilities, the authentication can still be misused, the dependencies can still be compromised, but they confine those risks to the places where they actually live, and they prevent the proliferation of weakly-controlled applications that is the single largest contributor to security failure at this scale of organisation.

==== The open-source stack, by category

A working application layer for a small-to-medium organisation can be assembled almost entirely from open-source components, each deployable from declarative configuration. The categories most organisations need are:

*Web servers and reverse proxies.* *Caddy* is the simplest default, automatic HTTPS through ACME, configuration in a single file, sensible defaults that produce a TLS 1.3 deployment without further effort. *nginx* is the alternative for higher-throughput environments or where existing configuration is in nginx's format. *HAProxy* for load balancing at scale.

*Mail.* *Postfix* for SMTP, *Dovecot* for IMAP, *Rspamd* for spam filtering. SPF, DKIM, and DMARC configured at the DNS level. The combination is mature, well-documented, and routinely operated by small teams.

*Directory and identity.* *Keycloak* as the identity provider, supporting OIDC and SAML, with *OpenLDAP* or *389 Directory Server* behind it where LDAP is required. *Authentik* as a lighter alternative for organisations that do not need Keycloak's full feature set.

*File and document collaboration.* *Nextcloud* for general file sharing and document editing. *Seafile* as an alternative oriented around sync rather than collaboration.

*Communication.* *Matrix* with *Synapse* or *Dendrite* for chat. *Jitsi* for video conferencing. *Mumble* for low-latency voice.

*Code and CI/CD.* *Gitea* or *Forgejo* for git hosting. *Drone* or *Woodpecker* or *Concourse* for continuous integration. *Argo CD* or *Flux* for continuous deployment to Kubernetes.

*Databases.* *PostgreSQL* as the relational default. *SQLite* for embedded use. *Redis* or *Valkey* for caching. *OpenSearch* or *Meilisearch* for search. *ClickHouse* for analytics. *MinIO* for S3-compatible object storage where on-premise is required.

*DNS.* *Unbound* as the validating resolver. *Knot DNS* or *PowerDNS* as authoritative servers for organisation-controlled zones. *dnsdist* in front for query routing where the deployment is large enough to need it.

*Observability.* Developed properly in its own chapter, but at this layer the relevant fact is that *Prometheus* and *Grafana* for metrics, *Loki* or *Elasticsearch* for logs, and *Jaeger* or *Tempo* for traces are all deployable as part of the same declarative infrastructure that runs everything else.

Every one of these has a NixOS module, an Ansible role, or both. Every one has reproducible builds available in some form. Every one can be operated by a team of single-digit size. The combination forms the kind of application substrate the introduction's principles describe, small, auditable, in-house, and reproducible from text.

==== What an architect decides at Layer 7

The principles reduce, at this layer, to a smaller number of decisions than the protocol catalogue might suggest. The decisions are:

*What applications the organisation runs, and why.* Each application is a trust decision. The list should be short. The justification for each should be explicit.

*How users and services authenticate to applications.* Federation through a central identity provider is the default. Per-application credential stores are the exception, used only where federation is structurally impossible.

*How applications authenticate to each other.* mTLS where both sides are operated by the organisation. Service tokens issued by the identity layer where mTLS is impractical. Long-lived static credentials are not used.

*How TLS is deployed.* TLS 1.3 only. Certificates issued automatically through ACME or an internal CA. Renewal is automatic. mTLS where the organisational topology supports it. No termination at midpoint appliances.

*How DNS is operated.* Internal validating resolver. Encrypted client-to-resolver path. DNSSEC on organisation-published zones.

*Where the boundary is between in-house and vendor.* Each SaaS subscription justified or rejected on its merits. The data that leaves the organisation's direct control catalogued and understood.

*How applications are deployed and operated.* Declarative configuration, version control, automated certificate management, secrets from a secrets store, logs and metrics into the shared observability pipeline. An application not meeting these criteria is brought into compliance or replaced.

*How the application supply chain is monitored.* SBOMs generated, fed into the vulnerability correlation pipeline, dependencies tracked, provenance verified.

The architecture that emerges from these decisions is one in which the application layer is the natural extension of everything below it: small, comprehensible, expressed as text, encrypted by default, authenticated explicitly, and operated as code. The applications are the place where the work the organisation actually does happens, and they are also the place where the cryptographic guarantees of every lower layer terminate. The architecture that takes this seriously treats the application layer not as a place where security products are deployed to compensate for risk, but as a place where the principles in the introduction continue to apply unchanged: fail-safe defaults, least privilege, complete mediation, economy of mechanism, and explicit trust boundaries. The application layer is where they are tested most directly. It is also where, when they are genuinely applied, they produce the most visible reward, an application stack the organisation understands, controls, and can defend.

